the largest public school district here  in the country has abruptly stopped in  person learning that's new york city  forcing more than a quarter million  students to stay home  new york city mayor bill de blasio  announced the move yesterday to make all  new york city public school virtual  again  leaving many parents scrambling this  comes as three percent of coronavirus  tests in the city come back positive  now that number has not been that high  since the spring mayor bill de blasio  joins us now first on cbs this morning  mr mayor thank you for joining us your  ears must be burning today  the parents are mad with you the kids  are upset  your critics are calling questioning  your competency for the job  help us understand your thinking here  gail look  my kids went to new york city public  schools i really do feel what parents  are going through i told them last week  we had to get ready for this possibility  parents are scrambling because it's  really tough to be a parent especially  in the age of the coronavirus but the  number one job i have is to keep people  safe  i said from the beginning we would be  led by the data and the science  i have the largest school system in  america  a lot of people said we would never be  able to reopen it but we did back in  september  this is a question how are you  justifying that three percent number  when the the data doesn't seem to  support it and neither neither do the  public health experts  i think that's what has people upset  disagree with that gail that the data is  very clear  that we have to keep our kids safe our  educators safe staff  we've had increasing rate obviously as  you said in your opening here in new  york city and everywhere around the  country  we set a clear standard we said if it  goes above 3 percent we're going to shut  down for a period of time but then we're  going to reopen  with a new set of additional health and  safety standards that's the key thing  before we're going to open again i think  the question for for parents and  for everybody right now is why three  percent the world health organization  says five percent the governor says he  wouldn't take action  uh as long as the state below nine  percent other cities and states around  the country have higher thresholds  you've chosen three percent you're  sticking to it  how come because we were the epicenter  of this crisis everyone knows that  we needed to bring our schools back in  the fall and we needed to show  parents and staff that they'd be safe so  we set a very stringent standard  and a whole lot of other health and  safety standards the distancing the face  mask for everyone  kids and adults alike and we proved that  schools would be safe but we said  if that rate went up higher we were  going to stop we were going to pause and  reset  our schools are going to come back but  they're going to come back with  additional safety standards  you're leaving the bars and the  restaurants open where it's been proven  that those are  super spreader places as opposed to the  schools  yeah gail the governor made clear  yesterday  that new york state is going to very  soon  be applying what they call an orange  zone standard to new york city and that  will  be closing uh indoor dining and a number  of other things so those restrictions  are coming very soon it's quite clear  but mayor in the meantime a lot of  parents are joking i guess i'll send my  kid to the bar today because that's  still open or maybe they can work out at  the gym pump a little iron  uh you know i want to know more about  your equation  here because the cost of this shutdown  we know are going to be significant it's  going to be significant in terms of  learning for children  the economy because parents can't work  domestic turmoil new york city  apartments are very small  that's the cost you've acknowledged  those costs can you quantify the benefit  here  tony the benefit is keeping people alive  and keeping people keeping people safe  look  we have to be clear about this history  we were the epicenter of the crisis and  a lot of people  truly believed it would never be safe  for our schools we come back until  there's something  we know you have to keep faith with  people and show will keep them safe  you're right on the we did not know back  in april but we do know now and there's  been an  evolution in the approach europe has  bars and restaurants closed but schools  are open  other states and president trump we have  to remember was calling for this in the  summer a lot of democrats were opposed  to it  but states have come around and said you  know we are going to prioritize schools  because that is so important  and so that's why this equation is so  important it's a cost benefit  you can't put any number at all on on  what the benefit's going to be and yet  we know the cost  sixty thousand kids don't even have  ipads to to learn at home  we have been providing free ipads for  every child in new york city your  chancellor said yesterday that they  don't have ipads for sixty thousand kids  it's really simple really clear  every child new york city needs one can  get a free ipad and any child doesn't  have it now we're going to get to them  right away and we've distributed over  300 000 we'll do more  but the bottom line is this safety and  health come first  we can and we will reopen our schools  but with an even higher and more  stringent standard  that's the bottom line new york city  schools will come back it's going to  require a lot more testing  but we will bring them back mr mayor  what about the quality of learning  my wife's a teacher at a charter school  here in the city they  a lot of teachers are concerned about  the virus and going in but they're also  concerned about kids falling behind and  increasingly say that's happening  a lot what do you do about that yeah  listen i'm really concerned about that  going all the way back to march when we  had to close originally  i think the key thing i'm a believer in  in-person education that's why i truly  want to get the schools back with the  right standards  and obviously once the vaccine is here  we're going to distribute it very  quickly in new york city and get our  schools back in a much  deeper way but that's not until the  earliest i think we have lost something  here with our kids  and what we've got to do is continue to  improve remote education continue to  make sure every kid has a device  needs them and get them back in the  classroom as quickly as we can safely do  it  was there an update was there an uptick  in coveted cases in schools  here in the city is that clearly the  upwards  the the challenge is everywhere in the  city it went from just a few weeks ago  gail  very isolated pockets in new york city  we're having serious upticks to now  unfortunately it's all five boroughs  numbers i'm talking about and of course  it's been affecting schools too we've  had a number of schools that have had to  be shut individually for that reason  that's why we have to get ahead of this  for safety's sake but we can put more  stringent standards in including a lot  more testing i think this is going to be  the key  a lot more testing school by school to  get them back and running yeah mr mayor  i think another thing i'd like to point  out that is upsetting parents is that  the  private schools are open so that  exacerbates inequality even more  and i want to call your attention to an  editorial by nick kristoff  generally a progressive columnist for  new york times who said that president  trump was right over the summer to call  for schools to reopen  because the science actually has come  around and supported that position  you were opposed then it seemed  political what do you say to people who  look at you holding to this three  percent which is more conservative than  anyone else in the country and they say  he's sticking to a political decision  it's politics again i don't think it's  political in the least  again it's ironic you're right president  trump wanted schools  open i generally disagreed with him  across the board but in this case  i believe from the beginning we had to  reopen and lots of folks thought  it couldn't be done safely in new york  city we proved it could be but we're  dealing with a new circumstance the way  you opened the broadcast was so clear  we're dealing with a new circumstance a  surge that we have to be very very  concerned about we got to reset this  equation  so i say to my fellow parents i don't  expect this to go for long i expect us  to come up with a new standard that's  more stringent we're going to ask a lot  of parents we're going to ask them to  get their kids tested a lot  but then we can come back that's what i  was running what is your timeline to  reopen do you have one  this next few days with the stay in new  york we're going to come up with a new  standards and announce them before  thanksgiving  and then i hope to be back in the next  few weeks but again i'm going to ask  a lot of parents to make sure kids are  tested very consistently i think that's  going to be the key  gail yeah right and then in the meantime  overwhelmingly black and brown school  district and low-income kids are going  to be back in very small apartments  for the foreseeable future that's why we  want again back in school they're trying  to look the right way the safe way  all right mayor de blasio thank you very  much

